## The model never sees a request

When you type into a chat, it feels like asking a question. To the model it is the start of a
document, and its only job is to guess what comes next (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" />). The prompt is the context (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />), and
every word in it pulls the probabilities of the next token one way or another (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-engineering">Prompt engineering</GlossaryPopover>
is choosing that text so that the answer you want becomes the likely continuation.

You can watch this happen with GPT-2, which has had no training to follow instructions. Change one
part of the prompt at a time:

<PromptVariants />

## Setting the scene

"The cat sat on the" ends with *floor* about 8% of the time. Add "It was bedtime," in front, and
*bed* jumps from about 7% to 22%. Put the cat at the beach, and *sand*, nearly impossible before,
becomes one of the top guesses. The shading shows why: attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />) lets the last token pull in
"kitchen" or "beach" from the start of the sentence.

## Making the answer the obvious next text

Asking GPT-2 "What is the capital of France?" gives *Paris* only half a percent; its top guess, at
34%, is a new line. Writing the prompt as the start of an answer, "The capital of France is", makes
*Paris* about six times as likely. The "Q: … A:" layout works the same way: after "A:", an answer is
what comes next.

Chat models were tuned so that a plain question is already followed by an answer (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />), so they
need less of this coaxing. The mechanism is the same: your words set up the document, and the model
continues it. GPT-3 showed how far a large enough model can go on this alone, doing new tasks from a
description and a few examples in the prompt, with no change to its weights.<Cite id="brown2020" />

<RecapCard>

- A prompt is the start of a document; the model predicts what comes next.
- Every word shifts the next-token probabilities, through attention to the earlier text.
- A good prompt makes the answer you want the most likely continuation.

</RecapCard>

